The client caps manual caching at 100 games ("to keep bulk caching from
generating too many RetroAchievements requests at once", docs/caching-games.md)
and derives the smart-cache limit from the same constant. On ROCKNIX the scan
is the way every game on the console comes to earn offline (fork #179,
D-RA-010), and the maintainer's call is that every game does (fork #184 note 6,
D-RA-014): "Why would there be a limit on how many games I can have stored for
offline play? I would want every game available for offline play to be able to
be played offline with the ability to unlock the achievements." The cap goes;
the request throttle (network.py: 0.3 s between requests, a cooldown per batch,
backoff on 429) stays, so a full-library scan is as polite as the capped one,
only longer.


--- a/linux/raofflineproxy/rom_browser.py
+++ b/linux/raofflineproxy/rom_browser.py
@@ -46,7 +46,10 @@ SUPPORTED_ARCHIVE_EXTENSIONS = {".zip", ".7z"}
 # which bundles a 7z reader (third_party/lzma-sdk), so it never reaches zipfile.
 ZIP_READABLE_ARCHIVE_EXTENSIONS = {".zip"}
 EXCLUDED_BROWSER_DIR_NAMES = {"Imgs"}
-MAX_CACHED_GAMES = 100
+# ROCKNIX: no cap on how many games the scan may cache (fork #184, D-RA-014);
+# upstream's 100 was a courtesy to the server, which the request throttle in
+# network.py keeps. Large enough that no library reaches it.
+MAX_CACHED_GAMES = 1000000
 MAX_SCAN_ENTRIES = 5000
 MAX_SCAN_DEPTH = 12
 # RetroAchievements adds hashes over time, so a "no match" is only cached long enough to
